1. Established the Project Foundation

We created the initial C++ project structure and shared data types.

The foundation includes:

CMakeLists.txt — Defines the project build configuration.

include/ghostbyte/types.hpp — Defines shared data structures and enumerations.

src/main.cpp — Provides the program entry point.

.gitignore — Excludes generated build files and other unwanted files.

Initial directories for implementation, tests, and documentation.

These files establish the structure needed to develop GhostByte as a modular C++ application.

2. Set Up Version Control

We initialized Git, created the initial commit, and pushed the project to GitHub.

Version control allows us to:

Track implementation changes.

Restore earlier versions when necessary.

Organize development into milestones.

Experiment without losing the project's history.

Maintain a record of the project's progress.

3. Established the Development Environment

Development has moved from an initially prepared native Windows toolchain to an Ubuntu terminal.

This affects how GhostByte will collect system memory statistics.

On Linux, the initial monitor will use /proc/meminfo, a virtual file that exposes memory information provided by the kernel.

This replaces the Windows-specific GlobalMemoryStatusEx approach considered earlier.

Development will continue in the existing repository.

