Understanding Each File and Folder

Root-Level Files

CMakeLists.txt — Build Configuration

CMake describes how the C++ project should be compiled and linked into an executable.

It specifies details such as:

The required C++ standard.

Source files included in the executable.

Header file locations.

Build targets and dependencies.

Test configuration, when added.

For example, when system_monitor.cpp is implemented, it must be included in the appropriate CMake target.

Think of this file as the build recipe for GhostByte.

README.md — Project Documentation

The README introduces the project to developers and visitors on GitHub.

It documents the project's purpose, architecture, setup instructions, current capabilities, limitations, and development roadmap.

It should clearly distinguish implemented functionality from planned functionality.

.gitignore — Excluded Files

The .gitignore file specifies files and directories Git should generally leave untracked.

Typical exclusions include:

Build directories.

Compiled executables and object files.

Temporary files.

Local IDE configuration.

Generated test artifacts.

This keeps the repository focused on source code and project assets.

include/ghostbyte/ — Interfaces and Shared Definitions

The include/ghostbyte/ directory contains header files (.hpp).

These files declare shared types, classes, and function interfaces that other components can use.

A header generally describes what a component exposes, while its corresponding .cpp file implements how it works.

types.hpp — Shared Data Types

This is one of the initial files created for GhostByte.

It defines common concepts used throughout the architecture.

MemorySnapshot

Stores a snapshot of memory statistics collected from the operating system.

Depending on the final implementation, it may contain values such as total memory, available memory, used memory, and timestamps.

PressureLevel

Represents the assessed memory-pressure level.

Possible values:

Healthy

Elevated

Critical

Unknown

ReclamationMode

Represents the intended response to memory pressure.

Possible values:

None

Conservative

Moderate

Aggressive

PolicyDecision

Stores the result of a policy evaluation, including the assessed pressure level, selected response, and an explanation for the decision.

These shared types help different components communicate using consistent representations.

Status: Initial data structures exist; their integration into a working controller remains future work.

system_monitor.hpp — Memory Monitor Interface

Defines how other components request system-memory measurements.

For example, the interface could expose a method such as:

MemorySnapshot capture();

The rest of GhostByte should not need to know how Linux-specific memory statistics are collected.

Keeping the interface independent of operating-system details makes the architecture easier to test and extend.

Status: Planned for the Linux implementation.

pressure_analyzer.hpp — Memory-Pressure Interface

Defines how GhostByte will interpret memory statistics and classify memory pressure.

For example, it may accept a MemorySnapshot and return a PressureLevel.

The implementation could initially use configurable thresholds based on available memory, followed by more advanced indicators if required.

Status: Planned.

policy_engine.hpp — Decision-Making Interface

Defines how GhostByte will select a response to the detected memory pressure.

The policy engine will consider the pressure assessment and any additional workload information supported by the implementation.

For example, it might choose a conservative response under elevated pressure and a stronger response under critical pressure.

These are intended policy behaviors, not implemented guarantees.

Status: Planned.

reclaimer.hpp — Reclamation Interface

Defines how a selected reclamation action will be carried out.

This component must distinguish between actions that merely simulate a decision and actions that actually change memory-management behavior.

Possible future approaches include supported Linux memory-management interfaces or controlled, explicitly configured reclamation experiments.

Reclaiming memory safely requires careful consideration of permissions, kernel behavior, workload impact, and system stability.

Status: Planned.

feedback_controller.hpp — Feedback Interface

Defines how GhostByte will compare system conditions before and after a reclamation action.

The feedback component will help evaluate whether a chosen action improved memory availability or caused undesirable side effects.

Its findings may later influence policy selection.

Status: Planned.

src/ — Implementation Files

The src/ directory contains the C++ implementations of GhostByte's components.

main.cpp — Program Entry Point

The main() function is where execution begins.

Initially, it can be used to verify that the project builds and runs correctly.

As the project develops, it will coordinate the major components:

Collect a memory snapshot.

Analyze memory pressure.

Request a policy decision.

Execute an enabled reclamation action.

Collect follow-up measurements.

Report the results.

The complete sequence will be introduced incrementally rather than implemented all at once.

system_monitor.cpp — Linux Memory Monitoring

Implements memory-statistics collection for Ubuntu/Linux.

The initial implementation will read /proc/meminfo and parse relevant fields, such as MemTotal, MemAvailable, and MemFree.

These values have different meanings. In particular, MemAvailable estimates memory available for new applications without requiring substantial swapping, whereas MemFree measures memory that is currently unused.

The monitor will translate the collected information into the shared MemorySnapshot structure.

pressure_analyzer.cpp — Pressure Classification

Implements the logic that translates memory statistics into a pressure classification.

An initial version may use simple thresholds. Later versions could consider additional indicators, such as changes in available memory and swapping activity.

The thresholds should be configurable and evaluated experimentally rather than treated as universal values.

policy_engine.cpp — Adaptive Decision Logic

Implements the rules used to choose a reclamation response.

The initial policy may use explicit rules based on pressure levels. A later version could incorporate workload characteristics and observed outcomes.

This separation makes it possible to compare different policies without rewriting the memory monitor.

reclaimer.cpp — Reclamation Actions

Implements the selected reclamation mechanisms.

This component will be developed cautiously, starting with safe experiments and controlled actions before considering more intrusive mechanisms.

The exact implementation will depend on the Linux interfaces selected for the project.

feedback_controller.cpp — Outcome Evaluation

Implements the process of comparing measurements before and after an action.

It may evaluate changes in available memory, pressure classification, swapping activity, and workload performance.

The objective is to determine whether a response was useful, ineffective, or harmful.

tests/ — Automated Tests

The tests/ directory will contain tests for individual components.

test_system_monitor.cpp

Checks whether memory statistics are collected and parsed correctly.

test_pressure_analyzer.cpp

Checks whether representative memory snapshots produce the expected pressure classifications.

test_policy_engine.cpp

Checks whether the policy selects the expected response for each pressure level and other supported inputs.

Unit tests allow us to verify individual components before integrating them into the full program.

docs/ — Technical Documentation

architecture.md

Documents the system architecture, component responsibilities, interfaces, and data flow.

experiments.md

Records benchmark methodology, test conditions, measured results, and observations from different reclamation policies.

Documenting experiments is important because a policy that appears effective in one workload may perform poorly in another.

build/ — Generated Build Artifacts

The build/ directory contains generated files produced during compilation.

For example, it may contain CMake configuration files, object files, and the compiled executable.

This directory is generated locally and should generally be excluded from version control.